 # Specific Model Architectures
 
-Also called: NeRF Architectures, Neural Radiance Field Models
+Also called: NeRF Architectures, Neural Radiance Field Variants
 
 ## Overview
-Specific Model Architectures refers to the family of neural network topologies and data structures used to represent the radiance field in differentiable volume rendering. While the canonical NeRF uses a monolithic Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) to map 6D coordinates to density and color, this family encompasses methods that modify the network topology, input representation, or underlying data structures to address limitations in rendering speed, memory consumption, generalization, dynamic scene modeling, and single-view synthesis. These architectures set themselves apart from the canonical baseline by introducing mechanisms such as sparse grids, tensor decompositions, transformer attention, or explicit geometric priors, enabling applications ranging from real-time rendering to 3D-aware image generation.
+Specific Model Architectures refers to the family of neural network designs that replace or augment the canonical Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) used in the original NeRF [Parent] to address limitations in rendering speed, geometric accuracy, dynamic scene modeling, and generalization. While the canonical architecture maps 6D coordinates to density and color using a single global MLP, this family introduces specialized structures such as sparse grids, hybrid explicit-implicit representations, multi-scale hierarchies, and decoupled physical property estimators. These methods set themselves apart by modifying the underlying data structures (e.g., from dense tensors to sparse voxel grids) or the network topology (e.g., adding CNNs, diffusion priors, or deformation fields) to solve specific problems like computational bottlenecks, misalignment artifacts, or the inability to handle dynamic content [S1, unofficial][S2, S3].
 
