 ## Background
-The canonical NeRF architecture, introduced by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 [S3], established the baseline of using a dense MLP to map 6D coordinates to density and color. Subsequent research has focused on modifying this architecture to address specific challenges such as rendering speed, memory efficiency, single-view synthesis, and dynamic scenes. Methods like [AdaNeRF](6195-adanerf.md) and [Sparse and Efficient Representations](9326-sparse-and-efficient-representations.md) aim to accelerate rendering by optimizing sampling and data structures. Single-view methods like [SinNeRF](6199-sinnerf.md) and [NeRDi](9112-nerdi.md) adapt the architecture to work with limited input data by incorporating external priors. Generative models like [pi-GAN](10298-pi-gan.md) and [GIRAFFE](11360-giraffe.md) extend the architecture to learn distributions over scenes rather than fitting single instances. The field continues to evolve with hybrid approaches combining transformers, diffusion models, and explicit geometric representations.
+The canonical NeRF architecture, introduced by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 [S3], established the paradigm of representing scenes as continuous volumetric functions parameterized by MLPs. However, the global, dense nature of the MLP leads to high computational costs and limited expressiveness for complex scenes. Subsequent research has focused on specific architectural modifications to address these issues. Early extensions like NeRF in the Wild [S1, unofficial] introduced embeddings to handle unconstrained photo collections. Later works explored sparse representations [S1, unofficial][S2], dynamic fields [S1, unofficial][S2], and hybrid explicit-implicit structures [S1, unofficial]. The field has also seen the integration of generative priors [S1, unofficial][S2], geometric constraints [S1, unofficial], and physical property decomposition [S1, unofficial][S2, S3] to enable applications such as single-view synthesis [S1, unofficial][S2], relighting [S1, unofficial][S3], and 3D object detection [S1, unofficial].
 
